        <!-- ==================================================================
             STEP 7: CONTROLLER XML IMPORT WARNINGS FAQ
             ================================================================== -->
        <article class="doc-step-card" id="import-warnings-faq">
          <div class="doc-step-header">
            <span class="doc-step-badge">Step 07</span>
            <h2 class="doc-step-title">Controller XML Import Warnings FAQ</h2>
          </div>

          <div class="doc-step-body">
            <p>
              When you import a controller XML file into RotaryCAM, the importer flags settings that can stall your rotary chuck, mess up pierce delays, or scale parts wrong. Find your exact warning below to see what it means on your table and what to fix before cutting.
            </p>

            <!-- 1. ArtSoft Mach3 (.xml) -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">ArtSoft Mach3 • Profile .xml Warnings</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">1. ArtSoft Mach3 (.xml) Import Warnings</h3>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-units" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Unrecognized or Missing <code class="mono">&lt;Units&gt;</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Your Mach3 XML did not specify inches or millimeters, so RotaryCAM defaulted Machine Units to <strong>mm</strong>. If your steps/unit in Motor Tuning are calibrated in inches, parts will cut 25.4x the wrong size.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Check <strong>Machine Units</strong> (<code class="mono">mm</code> vs. <code class="mono">in</code>) in the RotaryCAM Machines modal and match your Mach3 Motor Tuning units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" id="faq-mach3-slaved-a" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Motor 3 (A-Axis) Slaved to X / Y / Z (<code class="mono">Slave0=0/1/2</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Your table uses Motor 3 (A-axis) as the second motor on a dual-drive gantry (common on Bulltear Plasma and HVAC gantry tables) instead of a rotary chuck.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Flat-sheet DXF/SVG cuts work right away with zero changes. To cut round pipe on a dual-drive table, either switch <strong>Rotary Axis Mapping</strong> to <strong>3-Axis Y-Axis Wrap</strong> (and plug your rotary into Y), or unslave Motor 3 in Mach3 (<code class="mono">Config &rarr; Slave Axis</code>) if you move dual-drive slaving to hardware or the B-axis.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-a-angular" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">A-Axis Is Set to Linear Mode (<code class="mono">&lt;AAngular&gt;=0</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Mach3 treats your A-axis as linear millimeters or inches instead of degrees, so <code class="mono">G93</code> rotary speeds and chuck rotations will be way off.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> In Mach3, open <code class="mono">Config &rarr; General Config</code>, check <strong>A-Axis is Angular</strong>, save, and re-import your XML (or type your true A-axis <code class="mono">deg/min</code> limits directly into RotaryCAM).
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" id="faq-mach3-a-disabled" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">4th Axis Disabled (<code class="mono">&lt;AAxisOn&gt;=0</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> The A-axis output is turned off in your Mach3 profile.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Flat-sheet cuts work as-is with no changes. For 4-axis pipe cutting, enable Axis A in Mach3 (<code class="mono">Config &rarr; Ports &amp; Pins &rarr; Motor Outputs</code>), or switch RotaryCAM to <strong>3-Axis Y-Axis Wrap</strong>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-spindle-delay" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Spindle Spin-Up / Spin-Down Delay Active (<code class="mono">SpinDelayCW &gt; 0</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Mach3 adds an extra hidden pause after every <code class="mono">M3</code> torch fire on top of RotaryCAM's <code class="mono">G04</code> pierce delay, causing blown pierces and arc-loss faults.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Open <code class="mono">Config &rarr; Ports &amp; Pins &rarr; Spindle Setup</code> in Mach3 and set CW and CCW <strong>Delay Spin UP</strong> and <strong>Spin DOWN</strong> to <code class="mono">0</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" id="faq-mach3-screenset" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Mill / Spindle Screenset Detected</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Your Mach3 profile loads a router or mill screenset (<code class="mono">.set</code>) instead of a plasma screenset, so on-screen THC controls and Arc OK LEDs may be missing.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Load a plasma screenset in Mach3 (<code class="mono">View &rarr; Load Screens</code>), or verify your <code class="mono">M3/M5</code> torch output and THC pins before cutting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout danger" id="faq-mach3-dwell-ms" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">G04 Dwell in Milliseconds (<code class="mono">&lt;DwellinMilli&gt;=1</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Mach3 reads <code class="mono">G04 P0.5</code> as 0.5 milliseconds instead of 0.5 seconds, so your torch plunges before piercing through the plate.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Open <code class="mono">Config &rarr; General Config</code> in Mach3 and uncheck <strong>G04 Dwell in ms</strong> so Mach3 reads RotaryCAM's <code class="mono">G04 P</code> dwells in seconds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-plasma-cv" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Plasma Mode or CV Feedrate Active (<code class="mono">&lt;PlasmaMode&gt;=1</code> / <code class="mono">&lt;CVFeedOn&gt;=1</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Mach3's built-in Plasma Mode, CV Dist Tolerance, and CV Feedrate fight <code class="mono">G93</code> inverse-time rotary blending and cause choppy chuck motion.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> In Mach3 <code class="mono">Config &rarr; General Config</code>, uncheck <strong>Plasma Mode</strong> and <strong>CV Dist Tolerance</strong>, then open the <strong>Settings (Alt-6)</strong> tab and turn <strong>CV Feedrate OFF</strong>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-lookahead" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">LookAhead Too High (<code class="mono">&lt;LookAhead&gt; &gt; 200</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> LookAhead values above <code class="mono">200</code> lines in Mach3 can overflow the motion planner buffer and stutter on dense 4-axis tube saddle toolpaths.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Open <code class="mono">Config &rarr; General Config</code> in Mach3 and set <strong>LookAhead</strong> to <code class="mono">200</code> lines.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" id="faq-mach3-ij-mode" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Absolute IJ Arc Mode (<code class="mono">&lt;IJMode&gt;=0</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Your Mach3 default is Absolute IJ instead of Incremental IJ. RotaryCAM emits <code class="mono">G91.1</code> at the top of every file to force Incremental IJ, so normal runs cut fine, but mid-program restarts can drop back to Absolute IJ and throw crop-circle arcs.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Switch <strong>IJ Mode</strong> to <strong>Inc</strong> in Mach3 <code class="mono">Config &rarr; General Config</code> so mid-program <em>Run From Here</em> restarts stay safe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-safe-z" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Safe Z Height Zero or Negative (<code class="mono">m_SafeZ &lt;= 0</code>)</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Mach3 has no positive Safe Z retract height configured, so pausing or stopping mid-cut will not clear clamps or rotating tube corners.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Set a positive Safe Z height in Mach3 (<code class="mono">Config &rarr; Safe_Z Setup</code>) and verify your <strong>Safe Z Clearance</strong> in RotaryCAM clears your biggest tube diagonal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-mach3-ess-g28" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Warp9 ESS + <code class="mono">G28.1</code> Probing Warning</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Selecting <code class="mono">G28.1</code> touch-off probing with a Warp9 Ethernet SmoothStepper (ESS) often hangs the controller during floating-head probing.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Keep RotaryCAM's <strong>Touch-Off Strategy</strong> on <code class="mono">G31 Probe</code> (the Mach3 default) instead of <code class="mono">G28.1</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 2. FlashCut CNC (Setup.xml) -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider" style="margin-top: 2.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">FlashCut CNC • Setup.xml Warnings</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">2. FlashCut CNC (Setup.xml) Import Warnings</h3>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-flashcut-units" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Unrecognized or Missing <code class="mono">SystemUnits</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> RotaryCAM could not read <code class="mono">SystemUnits</code> from your FlashCut <code class="mono">Setup.xml</code> and defaulted Machine Units to <strong>mm</strong>.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Check <strong>Machine Units</strong> (<code class="mono">mm</code> vs. <code class="mono">in</code>) in the RotaryCAM Machines modal and match your FlashCut table units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" id="faq-flashcut-rotary-axis" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Missing or Unexpected <code class="mono">AxisParallel</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Your <code class="mono">Setup.xml</code> did not specify which gantry rail your rotary attachment runs along, so RotaryCAM defaulted <strong>Rotary Tube Axis</strong> to <strong>X</strong>.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> If your rotary chuck sits parallel to the Y gantry rail, select <strong>Parallel to Y-Axis</strong> in RotaryCAM's Machines modal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-flashcut-plasma-head" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Plasma Head ID Not Found in <code class="mono">&lt;Machine&gt;&lt;FabHeads&gt;</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> RotaryCAM could not match your requested Plasma Head ID or found no <code class="mono">*PlasmaHead</code> entries in your FlashCut XML, so head ID, arc transfer, and torch M-codes could not be auto-filled.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Pick your active plasma head from the dropdown in RotaryCAM, or type your <strong>Head ID</strong> (e.g. <code class="mono">Hplasma1</code>), <strong>Arc Transfer Input</strong>, and <code class="mono">M50/M51</code> M-codes manually.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-flashcut-zero-lift" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title"><code class="mono">ZeroLiftAxisPositionAfterTouchOff="False"</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> FlashCut is not resetting its Z lift coordinate to zero after <code class="mono">G605</code> touch-off probing. Standard 2D flat-sheet and round-pipe cuts work as-is, but 3D Ramp Lead-Ins and 4D square/angle tubes need a zeroed Z datum.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Enable <strong>Zero Lift Axis Position After Touch Off</strong> inside FlashCut (<code class="mono">Setup &rarr; FabHeads</code>) before running 3D ramps or 4D structural tubing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-flashcut-arc-transfer" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Missing <code class="mono">M101 I&lt;n&gt;</code> Arc Transfer Input</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> RotaryCAM could not find <code class="mono">M101 I&lt;n&gt;</code> in your Cut2D start macro (or had to guess from <code class="mono">InputLines</code>), so it cannot confirm which input waits for Arc OK.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Open FlashCut (<code class="mono">Setup &rarr; I/O &rarr; Inputs</code>), find your Arc Transfer / Arc OK input line number, and enter that number in RotaryCAM's FlashCut settings.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- 3. CSLab SimCNC 3.6 (config.xml) -->
            <div class="doc-section-divider cyan" style="margin-top: 2.25rem;">
              <div class="doc-section-kicker">CSLab SimCNC 3.6 • config.xml Warnings</div>
              <h3 class="doc-section-heading">3. CSLab SimCNC 3.6 (config.xml) Import Warnings</h3>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-simcnc-units" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Unrecognized or Missing <code class="mono">&lt;baseUnits&gt;</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Your SimCNC <code class="mono">config.xml</code> did not list <code class="mono">&lt;baseUnits&gt;</code>, so RotaryCAM defaulted Machine Units to <strong>mm</strong>.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Check <strong>Machine Units</strong> (<code class="mono">mm</code> vs. <code class="mono">in</code>) in RotaryCAM and confirm it matches your SimCNC Configuration units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout info" id="faq-simcnc-rotary-a" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title"><code class="mono">axis_3</code> (A-Axis) Disabled, Unbound, or Set to <code class="mono">linear</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> In SimCNC, <code class="mono">axis_3</code> (A) is turned off, has no MotionKit assigned, or is configured as a <code class="mono">linear</code> axis instead of <code class="mono">angular</code>.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Flat-sheet cuts work as-is. For 4-axis pipe cutting, open SimCNC (<code class="mono">Configuration &rarr; Axes</code>), enable <code class="mono">axis_3</code>, set its type to <strong>angular</strong>, bind a MotionKit, and re-import <code class="mono">config.xml</code>.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-simcnc-motionkit" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title">Missing <code class="mono">&lt;motionKit_N&gt;</code> or Mismatched Dual-Drive Gantry Limits</div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> Either a <code class="mono">&lt;motionKit_N&gt;</code> block is missing under <code class="mono">&lt;device&gt;&lt;modules&gt;</code>, or the two motors on a dual-drive gantry have different velocity, acceleration, or jerk limits (which can rack the gantry).<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Copy Max Velocity, Accel, and Jerk from SimCNC (<code class="mono">Configuration &rarr; Modules</code>) into RotaryCAM manually, and make sure both dual-drive gantry MotionKits use identical limits in SimCNC.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="doc-callout" id="faq-simcnc-ijk-mode" style="scroll-margin-top: 6rem;">
              <div class="doc-callout-title"><code class="mono">&lt;ijkMode&gt;</code> Not Set to <code class="mono">relative</code></div>
              <p>
                <strong>What it means:</strong> SimCNC is expecting Absolute IJK arc centers while RotaryCAM outputs Incremental (Relative) IJK arcs (and does not emit <code class="mono">G90.1</code>/<code class="mono">G91.1</code> on SimCNC), causing huge unwanted arc loops or soft-limit errors.<br />
                <strong>What to do:</strong> Open SimCNC (<code class="mono">Configuration &rarr; General</code>) and set <strong>Arc IJK Mode</strong> to <strong>Relative</strong>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>
{% endblock %}
